# Project board rules

Use only these `Status` values, in this order (you may move an item backward, e.g. `In Review` →
`Changes Requested`, but never invent a status outside this list):

```text
Backlog
Plan Review
Ready
In Progress
Blocked
In Review
Changes Requested
Done
Cancelled
```

The Project tracks these fields — keep the ones relevant to your current phase up to date, don't
leave them stale:

```text
Status, Priority, Size, Estimate, Iteration, Start date, Target date, Agent, Area, Risk,
Base Branch, Branch, PR URL, Last Agent Update, Handoff, Environment, Validation, Agent Run
```

Field vocabularies:

- `Validation`: `Not Run`, `Passed`, `Failed`, `Partial`, `Manual Required`, `Not Applicable`
- `Agent`: `Codex`, `Claude Code`, `Antigravity`, `Cursor`, `Gemini`, `ChatGPT`, `GitHub Copilot`,
  `Manual`, `Mixed`
- `Risk`: `Low`, `Medium`, `High`

Update fields with the helper scripts rather than guessing at the GraphQL/CLI calls yourself.
`scripts/project/create_agent_issue.sh` and `scripts/project/create_agent_pr.sh` create the
issue/PR with full sidebar metadata (assignee, labels, milestone, reviewer) and add it to the
Project in one call; `scripts/project/publish_agent_branch.sh` pushes the agent branch to `origin`
immediately and updates `Status`/`Branch`. For every later checkpoint, prefer
`scripts/project/sync_project_fields.sh <checkpoint> <issue-url> [text]` over calling
`project_set_status.sh`/`project_set_text.sh` directly — it's the single place that maps a workflow
checkpoint (`branch_published`, `implementation_started`, `blocked`, `validation_passed`,
`handoff_updated`, `pr_opened`, `changes_requested`, `done`, `cancelled`, etc.) to the right
`Status`/field update, so checkpoints stay consistent across agents:

```bash
scripts/project/create_agent_issue.sh --title "<title>" --body-file <path> \
  --agent "<name>" --area "<area>" --risk "<Low|Medium|High>" --environment "<env>" \
  [--parent <parent-issue>]
scripts/project/publish_agent_branch.sh --issue <issue-url> --slug <short-description>
scripts/project/sync_project_fields.sh <checkpoint> <issue-or-pr-url> [text]
scripts/project/create_agent_pr.sh --issue <issue-url> --base <base> --head <branch> \
  --title "<title>" --body-file <path> --agent "<name>" --area "<area>" \
  --risk "<Low|Medium|High>" --environment "<env>" --agent-run "<url>" --handoff "<note>"
```

Always pass `--agent`/`--area`/`--risk`/`--environment` explicitly to both scripts — don't rely on
the `AGENT_DEFAULT_*` fallback in `docs/ai/PROJECT_CONFIG.env` to fill them in; a blank `Agent`,
`Area`, `Risk`, or `Environment` field after either script ran is a bug, not expected behavior.
Pass `--parent <parent-issue>` on `create_agent_issue.sh` when an issue is one of a sub-task
breakdown (see `AGENTS.md` → "Splitting a task into sub-tasks") — it links the new issue as a real
GitHub sub-issue.

The lower-level scripts (`project_add_item.sh`, `project_set_status.sh`, `project_set_text.sh`)
still exist and are what the scripts above delegate to — call them directly only for one-off field
edits that don't correspond to a documented checkpoint.

Standard `status:`/`type:`/`risk:`/`agent:` labels are created and kept up to date by
`scripts/project/create_standard_labels.sh` (idempotent — safe to re-run); `create_agent_issue.sh`
and `create_agent_pr.sh` call it automatically, best-effort, before creating the issue/PR.

If `Status` becomes `Blocked`, always say why (in the issue, PR, or handoff file) — a blocked item
with no explanation isn't acceptable.
